    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d818">

                <pb facs="0786" n="776" xml:id="pg776"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.93/477</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Adviser on Political Relations</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornbeck</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-05-14">May 14, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary</hi>: I favor proceeding with an
                    exchange of <hi rendition="#italic">letters</hi> along the line
                        suggested.<anchor n="80" xml:id="d818-f1"/> You will note that FE<anchor
                        n="81" xml:id="d818-f2"/> reports that Mr. Pasvolsky favors such procedure.
                    In the record, FE takes no position. Mr. Hamilton has told me off the record
                    that he is “neutral.” Mr. Atcheson has told me off the record that he is
                    favorably disposed.</p>
                <p>The situation, in brief, is, it seems to me, this: The Chinese need assistance—of
                    many sorts; we have promised them assistance; the material assistance which we
                    expect to give them moves slowly and reaches them in a trickle; at this point
                    their Minister of Foreign Affairs and their Ambassador here ask us for something
                    in the way of moral encouragement, which it is within our power to give and the
                    giving of which would cost us nothing and commit us to little; we must either
                    give them this or in effect decline to give it. In my opinion it is better to
                    say and to do “Yes” than in effect to say and to do “No”.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>S[<hi rendition="#small-caps">tanley</hi>] K. H[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ornbeck</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
